refuted.evidence:
err = os.Remove(hookFile)
if err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("unable to remove %s hook %s: %s", hookType, hookFileName, err)
}

refuted.recommendation:
Restore the containment check in both `removeHooksCmd` and `editHooksCmd`, and make it robust by comparing cleaned absolute paths against the directory plus a separator rather than a bare `HasPrefix` (which would accept `/etc/uniget-evil`):

    hookFileAbs, err := filepath.Abs(filepath.Clean(hookFile))
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("unable to resolve hook file %s: %w", hookFile, err)
    }
    hooksDirAbs, err := filepath.Abs(hooksDir)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("unable to resolve hooks dir %s: %w", hooksDir, err)
    }
    if hookFileAbs != hooksDirAbs && !strings.HasPrefix(hookFileAbs, hooksDirAbs+string(os.PathSeparator)) {
        return fmt.Errorf("hook file %s is outside of hookDir %s", hookFile, hooksDir)
    }

Better still, reject the separator at the source, since a hook name is always a single filename: return an error when `args[0] != filepath.Base(args[0])`. Do not reinstate the `os.Lstat` "is a symlink" checks in their previous form; if symlinked hooks should be refused, test `fi.Mode()&os.ModeSymlink != 0` on the `Lstat` result instead of treating a nil error as proof of a symlink.

